<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="num-counting">
  <title>Counting and Natural Numbers</title>

  <p>
    Natural numbers are the numbers we use for counting: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, ...
  </p>
  <p>
    The question of whether 0 is a natural number is interesting.
    Historically, counting started at 1. But in modern mathematics, including 0 is often convenient.
  </p>
  <p>
    Some mathematicians write: <m>\mathbb{N} = \{1, 2, 3, \ldots\}</m>
  </p>
  <p>
    Others write: <m>\mathbb{N} = \{0, 1, 2, 3, \ldots\}</m>
  </p>
  <p>
    Both are used. In this book, we will use the second version because 0 appears naturally in many situations.
  </p>
</section>
